ry day kind of everybody has their  own flavor  or way to go about doing it so for our  analysis we use 200 milligrams of ground  flour  this could be scaled up and that really  depends on the requirements for your  state  so this is measured out into a 50  milliliter centrifuge tube  uh 10 milliliters of isopropyl alcohol  is added  again you know there's other extraction  solvents that can be used for this  i've seen you know a mixture of methanol  water ethanol  there's really a lot of different ways  to go about preparing these samples  so then the sample is vortexed for five  seconds and sonicated for five minutes  this is then repeated two additional  times for a total of three cycles  the sample is in centrifuge for five  minutes  an aliquot of the supernatant is taken  and diluted tenfold with solvent  and then the sample is vortexed and  transferred to a filter vial  and then injected onto the instrument  the subsequent analysis of these samples  is very straightforward  the top chromatogram is representative  of a cannabis flower sample  that was prepared by the previous method  and the bottom chromatogram is  representative  of a 16 cannabinoid standard analyzed in  solvent  just to confirm the retention times of  the real sample's peaks  both methods were analyzed on a raptor  arc 18  150 by 4.6 millimeter column  using an isocratic method the isochronic  method conditions are  25 water containing 5 millimolar  ammonium formate  0.1 percent formic acid and 75  acetonitrile with 0.1  formic acid detector used for this  is a uv vis at 228 nanometers and the  overall analysis time is 9 minutes  but what about new cannabinoids there's  over 150 phytocannabinoids that have  been discovered thus far  two of the most recent discoveries  include thcp  and cbdp which were discovered last year  the difference between their structures  and their parent structures  is the number of carbons contained on  the side chain  so here i have an example of a thc  structure  up top and so you can see this has five  carbons on that side chain  and the bottom structure is thcp and  this has seven carbons  so this actually means that as thcp has  a stronger affinity for the cb1 receptor  this actually results in thcp being 33  times stronger than thc  so you can see how this is a really  exciting discovery for the industry  but how can we go about detecting these  compounds  we were able to develop three different  methods monitoring 21 cannabinoids  including both cbdp and thcp to meet  various lab's needs  all three of these methods use the same  mobile phase conditions outlined in the  previous method  using an isocratic conditions and a  uv-vis detector  so all three methods use the raptor arc  18 phase  which is robust and perfect for high  throughput applications  the arc 18 is not a standard c18  it's hysterically 100 c18 and offers  similar but some unique selectivity  so the top method uses a 150 by 4.6  millimeter column dimension  and a 1.5 mil per minute flow rate  the overall run time is 12 minutes and  this is a great  rugged reliable choice the column  dimension  actually makes it so it's transferable  to older instrumentations that may not  be  optimized to control extra column  volumes  the middle chromatogram uses a 150 by  2.1 millimeter column dimension  and a 0.4 mil per minute flow rate  the overall run time for this method is  10 minutes cutting down  on the overall analysis time from the  previous method  it also uses significantly less solvent  and less sample  and we refer to this method as our  solvent saving method  so the bottom chromatogram uses a 100 by  3 millimeter column dimension  and a 1.8 micron particle with a flow  rate of 1 mil per minute  the overall analysis time for this is  five minutes making it the most  rapid of the three methods so some  considerations need to be taken  into your system's extra column volume  before choosing the  middle or bottom column dimensions  now let's move on to another sample type  concentrates  concentrates come in many different  forms consistencies and compositions  depending on how they're prepared it  kind of depends on what will be found in  the final product  so it could be prepared dry water-based  solvent-based or co2  so the two most common classes that are  found in concentrates  are cannabinoids which are potency  tested by lc  and terpenes which are typically  monitored by gc analysis  uh so since this is mainly an lcuv uh  presentation we're just gonna stick to  talking about the cannabinoids of these  so for sample preparation it's pretty  straightforward 20 milligrams of  concentrate  is measured out into a 50 milliliter  centrifuge tube  10 milliliters of ethanol is added the  sample is then vortexed for 5 seconds  and sonicated for five minutes  this cycle is repeated two additional  times for a total of three cycles  then the sample is centrifuged and an  aliquot of the supernatant is taken and  diluted 50-fold with solvent  and then the sample is vortexed and  filtered using a filter vial  since we're discussing concentrate  samples let's touch a bit on thc  isomers there's a total of 30 different  isomeric forms of thc that have been  discovered thus far  i've listed a few up here of current  interest  of course everyone knows delta 9 thc  it's the main  psychoactive component found in cannabis  delta 8 thc is another analog and is  also psychoactive  but it's potentially less potent the  initial passing of the farm bill  in united states didn't initially  include language for delta athc  allowed it to kind of fly under the  radar but it's since become illegal  to convert cbd to delta 8 thc in several  states  xothc is typically not found naturally  in cannabis but it could potentially be  an indication of synthetic thc  or the byproduct of manufacturing  processes of concentrate preparations  delta 10 thc is becoming a hot topic and  is thought to not have psychoactive  effects  but testing's somewhat limited and it  could have diluted psychoactive effects  so here a method has been developed to  baseline separate these four thc  isomers of interest in just under four  minutes  so this method uses a raptor  fluorophenyl 100 by three millimeter  column  um the flow rate for this is point eight  milliliters per minute with an isocratic  mobile phase conditions consisting of a  five millimolar ammonium formate plus  0.1  formic acid in water and for our organic  mobile phase 0.1 percent formic acid  in methanol so by using this method to  look at thc concentrates  uh labs can cut back on instrument time  due to the rapid analysis  but also save money by using methanol as  opposed to acetonitrile  some cannabis matrices rely on sample  preparation more so than  others there's so many different things  that cannabis has been added to  that are now available on the market  name a personal care product or type of  edible  and i bet you can find it infused with  cannabinoids  this makes it difficult for analytical  chemists to come up with methods for  each type of matrix  but it's important to remember all the  tools available in your analytical  toolbox  some of the available options for  cleaning up a matrix include things like  solid phase extraction or spe  centrifuging filtration  liquid liquid extraction catchers  dispersed solid phase extraction or dspe  and precipitation if you're wanting to  look at low levels of compounds  maybe something like pesticides or  mycotoxins in cannabis flower  you could opt for a pre-concentration  step like spe  to reach your desired limits of  detection  and then another tool that you could  choose maybe if you found a wonderful  extraction solvent for your analytes of  interest  but it's not compatible with your lc  mobile phases you can always dry down  the sample and reconstitute it with a  more compatible solvent  one set of samples that relies heavily  on sample preparation  are cbd infused lotion bombs and creams  these samples 