e prep a  half of a gram of sample was added to a  50 milliliter centrifuge tube  and uh five milliliters of methyl  tert-butyl ether was  added and then 50 microliters of the  sample  after it was vortex 50 microliters of  the sample was transferred to  a centrifuge tube and the solvent was  dried off  and then the sample was reconstituted  with solvent and vortexed  and then centrifuged for 30 minutes at 4  degrees celsius  this was transferred to an lc vial and  injected onto the instrument  so for the instrument conditions again  we're using a raptor arc 18 column the  150 by 4.6 millimeter dimension  we're using a guard column cartridge for  this just to help protect our analytical  column  ensure uh that we are getting the full  use out of this column's lifetime  the overall flow rate of this is 1.5  milliliters per minute  and we're using the same isocratic  mobile phase conditions as previously  outlined  so pictured here we have our lotion balm  or a cream sample dissolved in our  extraction solvent our methyl terpetyl  ether  you can see that that's not completely  soluble  just about all the samples had some type  of precipitation  or flocculation in it um the percent  recovery for the  these experiments ranged from uh 75  percent  to 95 so there is some room for  improvement here  so listed here we have our samples in  the left hand column  our experimentally calculated milligrams  of cbd per  bottle and then the standard deviation  for these experiments  the label value and our calculated  percent  difference from the label value so  highlighted in red  here i just have anything over 20  we want to stay between zero to twenty  percent four percent difference if we  can  so we have two of these uh samples one  lotion and one cream  that is above our target point  some initial solvent scouting for this  so we  wanted to find a universal solvent  that's going to dissolve  all of these different samples as much  as possible  so if they can't completely dissolve the  sample  we want as small as particles as  possible just to increase that surface  area  so a number of different solvents were  tested  these include methanol toluene hexane  ethyl acetate ipa mtbe acetone  acetonitrile and isooctane co-solvents  were also tested  this is just qualitatively seeing what  will dissolve  best ultimately we came up with a  mixture of  ipa acn and hexane to dissolve the  samples  as much as possible so down at the  bottom  in the pictures we have the mtbe samples  labeled in red  and then labeled in the white numbers is  going to be our  mixture of co-solvents so you can see  for the most part these samples are  dissolved  pretty well a lot better than with mtbe  alone  now samples 4 5 and 6 are going to be  our  bomb samples so that's likely going to  be bee's wax down at the bottom  now let's start with sample preparation  optimization  so now we're going to start off by uh  see if we can optimize the centrifuging  and winterization step  so previously we were doing this for 30  minutes  so we want to test the percent  recoveries in our new solvent  by looking at them at five minutes 10  minutes 15  and 30 of centrifuging and winterization  and then seeing if we could maybe just  do away with that stuff  altogether and just use a filter vial  with no centrifuging and no  winterization  here we have our results from those  experiments  our percent recovery on that y-axis  so we're looking for a range from 110  to 9 or 90 to 110 on that x-axis we have  each of our seven  samples that were tested and then the  key over here  uh in the lighter blue color is going to  be our original method so  our mtbe solvent uh winterizing and  centrifuging for  30 minutes and then below that is going  to be our mixture of solvents  with our various amounts of time or  completely doing away with the  centrifuging  and winterization altogether so looking  at these results  overall the mixture with the filter vial  had the best results  if you look in between the 90 to 110  uh lines here only really lotion number  one was a little bit out of range  now let's move on to the quantitation of  cbd  so just to kind of throw a little bit of  a monkey wrench into this we wanted to  add some additional cbd containing  samples  just to see how well our method worked  so we're adding four additional samples  uh two lotion samples one cream and one  balm  with varying concentrations of infused  cbd  and wildly different ingredients so  let's see how  our method that has been previously  developed holds up  so our sample prep right now is half a  gram of sample with five milliliters of  our mixture of co-solvents we vortex  that and then centrifuge for five  minutes  take an aliquot of the supernatant and  transfer that to a vial  and dry down the solvent then we  reconstitute that sample  uh with one milliliter of solvent and  use the filter vial before injecting  onto the instrument  so these are our results for our  developed method  with our co-solvents for extraction  on the left hand side this is going to  be our 11 different  samples that we have beside that is  going to be the  experimentally calculated milligrams of  cbd  per bottle and then followed by the  standard deviation  and our label value and then finally in  the right hand column is going to be our  percent difference  from the label value so anything  highlighted in red is anything  over 20 so if you look at this data  you can start to notice something  interesting so  out of the five samples that  have high percent difference from the  label value  four of these are actually bomb samples  since bombs had a large percent  difference from the label value  um we wanted to go back and reevaluate  our extraction solvent so during the  preliminary  solvent scouting we actually observed  that toluene dissolved the bomb samples  really well  and so pictured below is each of the  samples dissolved  in toluene and 4 5 6 and 11  are going to be bomb samples  so here highlighted in the blue box are  going to be our results from using  toluene as a solvent  and in this pinky purple box are going  to be our previous results  with our co-solvent mixture so  looking at this data when we look at the  percent difference value  it actually got a lot worse even  compared to  our our previous analysis using our  co-solvents  so now it's time to take kind of a new  approach to this  so instead of thinking about what's  going to completely dissolve these  samples the best  let's find something that cbd will  actually extract into  really well so we know cbd is very  soluble in methanol  so let's try using methanol as an  extraction solvent  even though in our preliminary tests we  know that it's not going to completely  dissolve the samples  and we are going to get some  flocculation  so now using methanol as our extraction  solvent  we can run through the process uh  basically weighing out our half gram  of lotion bomb or cream adding five  milliliters of  methanol i actually found a paper um  in a literature research that used uh 95  5 methanol water with 0.1 percent formic  acid  and they seem to have really good  results with this so i decided to  try that and so after adding the solvent  the sample is vortexed and centrifuged  and then an aliquot is transferred to a  vial and dried down  a one milliliter of solvent is added and  vortexed  and then it is transferred to a filter  vial before being injected onto  the instrument here we have  our experimentally determined  concentration of samples for  the methanol solvent we have our  experimentally calculated cbd  per bottle our standard deviation and  our percent  difference for the label value so we can  see this is an  improvement on some of our previous  solvents  we still have a few of our samples that  are  higher than our 20 cut off  so here i've gathered uh all of the data  for the extraction solvents that we've  used  thus far so if you remember back for  the mtbe data 