r sample type  concentrates  concentrates come in many different  forms consistencies and compositions  depending on how they're prepared it  kind of depends on what will be found in  the final product  so it could be prepared dry water-based  solvent-based or co2  so the two most common classes that are  found in concentrates  are cannabinoids which are potency  tested by lc  and terpenes which are typically  monitored by gc analysis  uh so since this is mainly an lcuv uh  presentation we're just gonna stick to  talking about the cannabinoids of these  so for sample preparation it's pretty  straightforward 20 milligrams of  concentrate  is measured out into a 50 milliliter  centrifuge tube  10 milliliters of ethanol is added the  sample is then vortexed for 5 seconds  and sonicated for five minutes  this cycle is repeated two additional  times for a total of three cycles  then the sample is centrifuged and an  aliquot of the supernatant is taken and  diluted 50-fold with solvent  and then the sample is vortexed and  filtered using a filter vial  since we're discussing concentrate  samples let's touch a bit on thc  isomers there's a total of 30 different  isomeric forms of thc that have been  discovered thus far  i've listed a few up here of current  interest  of course everyone knows delta 9 thc  it's the main  psychoactive component found in cannabis  delta 8 thc is another analog and is  also psychoactive  but it's potentially less potent the  initial passing of the farm bill  in united states didn't initially  include language for delta athc  allowed it to kind of fly under the  radar but it's since become illegal  to convert cbd to delta 8 thc in several  states  xothc is typically not found naturally  in cannabis but it could potentially be  an indication of synthetic thc  or the byproduct of manufacturing  processes of concentrate preparations  delta 10 thc is becoming a hot topic and  is thought to not have psychoactive  effects  but testing's somewhat limited and it  could have diluted psychoactive effects  so here a method has been developed to  baseline separate these four thc  isomers of interest in just under four  minutes  so this method uses a raptor  fluorophenyl 100 by three millimeter  column  um the flow rate for this is point eight  milliliters per minute with an isocratic  mobile phase conditions consisting of a  five millimolar ammonium formate plus  0.1  formic acid in water and for our organic  mobile phase 0.1 percent formic acid  in methanol so by using this method to  look at thc concentrates  uh labs can cut back on instrument time  due to the rapid analysis  but also save money by using methanol as  opposed to acetonitrile  some cannabis matrices rely on sample  preparation more so than  others there's so many different things  that cannabis has been added to  that are now available on the market  name a personal care product or type of  edible  and i bet you can find it infused with  cannabinoids  this makes it difficult for analytical  chemists to come up with methods for  each type of matrix  but it's important to remember all the  tools available in your analytical  toolbox  some of the available options for  cleaning up a matrix include things like  solid phase extraction or spe  centrifuging filtration  liquid liquid extraction catchers  dispersed solid phase extraction or dspe  and precipitation if you're wanting to  look at low levels of compounds  maybe something like pesticides or  mycotoxins in cannabis flower  you could opt for a pre-concentration  step like spe  to reach your desired limits of  detection  and then another tool that you could  choose maybe if you found a wonderful  extraction solvent for your analytes of  interest  but it's not compatible with your lc  mobile phases you can always dry down  the sample and reconstitute it with a  more compatible solvent  one set of samples that relies heavily  on sample preparation  are cbd infused lotion bombs and creams  these samples contain many different  components  i've listed a few of the most abundant  ingredients up here for each sample type  but what really makes these challenging  is that even you know lotion to lotion  sample or bondo bomb sample  the ingredients can vary wildly and so  it really makes it challenging to come  up with a method to be inclusive  for all of these types of matrices  so just to name a few of the top  ingredients of some of the samples we  looked at  for lotions they had things like aloe  vera  water shea butter fatty acids plant  extracts fragrances  and many many more bombs bombs are going  to be  contained beeswax that's kind of what  gives it that waxy consistency  of course they have many other  ingredients in there fatty acids esters  of fatty acids triglycerides  a lot of them have menthol have cream  ingredients so these are going to have  less water than lotions and typically be  thicker  they'll have emulsifiers glycerides  fatty acids  among other things so when developing a  method  for these types of samples it's  important to find a balance between the  solubility of  the matrix and the extractability of  your analytes of interest  so now we're going to do a deep dive  into the method development  for these sample sets so the overall  goal of these experiments was first to  find a universal extraction solvent  for all lotion bombs and creams that are  infused with cbd  so we want to simplify the sample  preparation and make sure  any steps that we put into the sample  preparation procedure  have purpose and we're not doing them  for too long or too short  then we want to develop a method to  accurately quantitate cbd  in these samples and then lastly we want  to make sure that we're producing  a clean extract and that this extract  won't  dirty the instrument or the column over  time  so a jumping off point for these samples  we have a starting sample preparation  procedure and some starting instrument  conditions  so to start off with the sample prep a  half of a gram of sample was added to a  50 milliliter centrifuge tube  and uh five milliliters of methyl  tert-butyl ether was  added and then 50 microliters of the  sample  after it was vortex 50 microliters of  the sample was transferred to  a centrifuge tube and the solvent was  dried off  and then the sample was reconstituted  with solvent and vortexed  and then centrifuged for 30 minutes at 4  degrees celsius  this was transferred to an lc vial and  injected onto the instrument  so for the instrument conditions again  we're using a raptor arc 18 column the  150 by 4.6 millimeter dimension  we're using a guard column cartridge for  this just to help protect our analytical  column  ensure uh that we are getting the full  use out of this column's lifetime  the overall flow rate of this is 1.5  milliliters per minute  and we're using the same isocratic  mobile phase conditions as previously  outlined  so pictured here we have our lotion balm  or a cream sample dissolved in our  extraction solvent our methyl terpetyl  ether  you can see that that's not completely  soluble  just about all the samples had some type  of precipitation  or flocculation in it um the percent  recovery for the  these experiments ranged from uh 75  percent  to 95 so there is some room for  improvement here  so listed here we have our samples in  the left hand column  our experimentally calculated milligrams  of cbd per  bottle and then the standard deviation  for these experiments  the label value and our calculated  percent  difference from the label value so  highlighted in red  here i just have anything over 20  we want to stay between zero to twenty  percent four percent difference if we  can  so we have two of these uh samples one  lotion and one cream  that is above our target point  some initial solvent scouting for this  so we  wanted to find a universal solvent  that's going to dissolve  all of these different samples as much  as possible  so if they can't completely dissolve the  sample  we want as small as particles as  possible just to increase that surface  area  so a number of different solvents were  tested  these include methanol toluene hexane  ethyl acetate ipa mtbe acetone  acetonitrile and isooctane co-solvents  were also tested  this is just qualitatively seeing what  will dissolve  best ultimately we came up with a  mixture of  ipa acn and hexane to dissolve the  samples  as much as possible so down at the  bottom  in the pictures we have the mtbe samples  labeled in red  and then labeled in the white numbers is  going to be our  mixture of co-solvents so you can see  for the most part these samples are  dissolved  pretty well a lot better than with mtbe  alone  now samples 4 5 and 6 are going to be  our  bomb samples so that's likely going to  be bee's wax down at the bottom  now let's start with sample preparation  optimization  so now we're going to start off by uh  see if we can optimize the centrifuging  and winterization step  so previously we were doing this for 30  minutes  so we want to test the percent  recoveries in our new solvent  by looking at them at five minutes 10  minutes 15  and 30 of centrifuging and winterization  and then seeing if we could maybe just  do away with that stuff  altogether and just use a filter vial  with no centrifuging and no  winterization  here we have our results from those  experiments  our percent recovery on that y-axis  so we're looking for a range from 110  to 9 or 90 to 110 on that x-axis we have  each of our seven  samples that were tested and then the  key over here  uh in the lighter blue color is going to  be our original method so  our mtbe solvent uh winterizing and  centrifuging for  30 minutes and then below that is going  to be our mixture of solvents  with our various amounts of time or  completely doing away with the  centrifuging  and winterization altogether so looking  at these results  overall the mixture with the filter vial  had the best results  if you look in between the 90 to 110  uh lines here only really lotion number  one was a little bit out of range  now let's move on to the quantitation of  cbd  so just to kind of throw a little bit of  a monkey wrench into this we wanted to  add some additional cbd containing  samples  just to see how well our method worked  so we're adding four additional samples  uh two lotion samples one cream and one  balm  with varying concentrations of infused  cbd  and wildly different ingredients so  let's see how  our method that has been previously  developed holds up  so our sample prep right now is half a  gram of sample with five milliliters of  our mixture of co-solvents we vortex  that and then centrifuge for five  minutes  take an aliquot of the supernatant and  transfer that to a vial  and dry down the solvent then we  reconstitute that sample  uh with one milliliter of solvent and  use the filter vial before injecting  onto the instrument  so these are our results for our  developed method  with our co-solvents for extraction  on the left hand side this is going to  be our 11 different  samples that we have beside that is  going to be the  experimentally calculated milligrams of  cbd  per bottle and then followed by the  standard deviation  and our label value and then finally in  the right hand column is going to be our  percent difference  from the label value so anything  highlighted in red is anything  over 20 so if you look at this data  you can start to notice something  interesting so  out of the five samples that  have high percent difference from the  label value  four of these are actually bomb samples  since bombs had a large percent  difference from the label value  um we wanted to go back and reevaluate  our extraction solvent so during the  preliminary  solvent scouting we actually observed  that toluene dissolved the bomb samples  really well  and so pictured below is each of the  samples dissolved  in toluene and 4 5 6 and 11  are going to be bomb samples  so here highlighted in the blue box are  going to be our results from using  toluene as a solvent  and in this pinky purple box are going  to be our previous results  with our co-solvent mixture so  looking at this data when we look at the  percent difference value  it actually got a lot worse even  compared to  our our previous analysis using our  co-solvents  so now it's time to take kind of a new  approach to this  so instead of thinking about what's  going to completely dissolve these  samples the best  let's find something that cbd will  actually extract into  really well so we know cbd is very  soluble in methanol  so let's try using methanol as an  extraction solvent  even though in our preliminary tests we  know that it's not going to completely  dissolve the samples  and we are going to get some  flocculation  so now using methanol as our extraction  solvent  we can run through the process uh  basically weighing out our half gram  of lotion bomb or cream adding five  milliliters of  methanol i actually found a paper um  in a literature research that used uh 95  5 methanol water with 0.1 percent formic  acid  and they seem to have really good  results with this so i decided to  try that and so after adding the solvent  the sample is vortexed and centrifuged  and then an aliquot is transferred to a  vial and dried down  a one milliliter of solvent is added and  vortexed  and then it is transferred to a filter  vial before being injected onto  the instrument here we have  our experimentally determined  concentration of samples for  the methanol solvent we have our  experimentally calculated cbd  per bottle our standard deviation and  our percent  difference for the label value so we can  see this is an  improvement on some of our previous  solvents  we still have a few of our samples that  are  higher than our 20 cut off  so here i've gathered uh all of the data  for the extraction solvents that we've  used  thus far so if you remember back for  the mtbe data we only had samples one  through six when we were doing the mtbe  solvent so we don't have data for  samples eight through 11. but  all the rest of the data is compiled  here so on the  y-axis we have percent difference and on  the x-axis is each  number sample so when we look at this we  can start to see  that um you know obviously toluene was  the worst extraction solvent that we  tried  um the majority of these looked really  really bad  um but whenever you start to look at  mtbe and methanol together you can kind  of get  get to see some really good data going  on here so  uh for state ball number one mtbe looked  good  for state one number two methanol looked  good for sample number three  methanol sample four mtbe  five really could go either way uh six  mtbe looks a little bit better and then  we don't have that data for the rest of  these but methanol looked pretty good  for the majority of these except for our  sample number 10.  so between those two solvents most of  the samples could get values within a 20  difference of that label value so we  want to go through the sample  preparation procedure again  and perform it with mtbe just so we have  data for all the samples but also  perform it with  mtve methanol and a 50 50 mixture for  the extraction solvent  as this could very possibly be our  universal solvent that we're looking for  so these are our results for our  mixture of solvents our methanol mtbe at  a 50 50 mixture  so looking at this data again we have  the  sample name our experimentally  calculated cbd per bottle  standard deviation and percent  difference of label value  so lotion number one and lotion number  10 are pretty high  but the rest of this data looks is  starting to look pretty good  um unfortunately at this point sample  number six was consumed and we couldn't  replenish that sample  so we just kind of had to drop that from  the analysis  so putting all this data together um  again looking at these percent  difference graphs for each solvent  tested for each sample  again we have our percent difference on  our y-axis  and our sample number on the x-axis  um mtbe data was replaced with the most  recent of data  um whenever we went back and redid all  of that  so you can see that uh the methanol mtve  at a 5050 mixture the the green bars  are looking pretty good for the majority  of these samples really  only sample ten and one need a little  bit of work  so let's go back and optimize our sample  preparation procedure  and see if we can work with sample  number one and sample number 10  and see if we can get these samples  looking good by optimizing our procedure  so using a half gram of lotion bomb or  cream again  adding our co-solvent uh methyl terbutyl  ether methanol  mixture and then vortexing we're going  to do  a step in there where we try and  optimize  time on shaker table so after we add  everything together we're gonna do  no time on shaker table five ten or  fifteen minutes  and then we'll centrifuge it for five  minutes uh take an aliquot of the  supernatant and dry off the solvent  reconstitute it with a different solvent  and then we're going to put that  centrifuging back in and see  if that will start to help with these  two samples  so we'll test this at 0 5 and 10 minutes  before transferring an aliquot into a  filter vial  and injecting that onto the instrument  so these are the results for our  previously outlined experiments  so up top is the bar graph for lotion  number one  and at the bottom the blue one is the  bar  graph for lotion number 10. on the  y-axis is calculated milligrams of cbd  per bottle  and that green dotted line is going to  be the  actually reported value so the the value  that's on the bottle  on our x-axis in the smaller subgroup we  have  no centrifuging five minutes of  centrifuging and  ten minutes and then in the larger uh  sub  portion of the x-axis we have time on  shaker table so no shaker table  five minutes ten minutes and fifteen so  when looking at this data you can start  to see some really interesting  trends going on here so basically  time on shaker table had the most effect  with no time on shaker table having some  of the  data that was largest away from the  actually reported values so overall  centrifuging didn't have much effect  between the  the subsets whether you did no  centrifuging five minutes or ten minutes  so ultimately five minutes on the shaker  table  and no centrifuging was added to our  sample preparation procedure  so our final sample preparation  procedure for these cbd  infused lotion bombs and creams was half  a gram of sample  with five milliliters of our methyl  tert-butyl ether and methanol 50 50  mixture added  sample is vortexed and total dissolved  as much as possible  it will not dissolve all the way for  some of these samples  and then the samples are put on a shaker  table for five minutes  and centrifuged and then an aliquot is  transferred to a vial and the solvent  dried  off then the sample is reconstituted  with solvent  and filtered with a filter vial so this  used a number of sample preparation  techniques including using an extraction  solvent centrifuging drying off the  solvent and reconstituting with a  different one  and filtration  so the analysis of these samples is  pretty straightforward  raptor rkt and 150 by 4.6 millimeter  column is used  with an isocratic method and an overall  five-minute analysis time  the mobile phases include the same  isochronic mobile phases as previously  outlined for  cannabis flower it's important to always  use a guard cartridge when analyzing  real samples to help protect your  analytical column  and improve the lifetime a  representative chromatogram can be seen  for lotion  at the top a bomb sample in the middle  and a cream sample at the bottom so the  cbd is  peak is alluding a little bit after  three minutes  and the overall analysis time for these  is five minutes just to ensure that  everything is eluding off the column  from the matrix  and there's no excessive buildup  so here we have our results from our  final separate sample preparation  method so our percent recovery is  ranging  from 90 to 108 percent  and our percent difference from our  reported value  ranges from 0 to 20.  so in summary it can be beneficial when  potency testing to have  tailored methods for each type of matrix  it can cost time in the beginning to  develop these methods  but by using tailored methods for each  type of matrix  labs can really save instrument time and  ensure that the best quality data is  being obtained  it's also important to remember that  different labs have different needs  and while some labs may be most  interested in saving money on mobile  phase usage  you know others may be most interested  in shortest  methods and highest throughput of  samples  and then finally sample preparation is  an important step in the method  development process  and it's really essential for complex  matrices as we saw for  our lotion bomb and cream samples this  is going to be the key  to keeping instruments up and running  and obtaining reliable and reproducible  data  as well as maximizing the lifetime of  the analytical column  so i'd like to thank you for your time  today and for joining me  and i'd like to open it up to any  questions from the audience  um if you think of a question later feel  free to  uh send me an email i've listed my email  here on the slide
 

